    "media:imagetext": "-2-\nto the \"editerranean, Basra was one of the principal ports of call\nfor tankers and was especialiy important during the war when the\n\"Desert Fox\" Rommel all but closed the Mediterranean and the Suez\nCanal to allied shipping. Kuwait is a most interesting and little\nknown Sheikdom. It has\nrecently become such an important oil source\nthat P.A.A. is seeking authority from the C.A.B. to land there.\nSheik (pronounced Shake) now has an income of $80,000 per day in oil\nroyalties or •20¢ per barrel from 400,000 barrels of oil. He lives\nin a rather glorified mud house in a small village of the same con-\nstruction.\nHis one extravagance is a big Packard presented to him\nby one of the oil companies and a rather well-stocked harem of his\nown selection.\nThe people of Kuwait heretofore and even now thrive\non gold. The latter is flown into Kuwait from ell over the world.\nIn fact, the airline on which we are now flying, which is one-third\nowned by P.A.A., does a big business in the transport of gold to\nKuwait. All is perfectly legal until the gold arrives there.\nthe dhows, that picturesque type of vessel with a triangular sail,\ncarry the gold down the Persian Gulf and across the Indian Ocean\nThere in some secluded bay or inlet the gold\nis smuggled into India. Fach of these strange craft may carry as\nmuch as $500,000-worth of gold. Probably the skipper is the only\nman on board who can read or write but the twenty man crew all share\nin the profits and are quite reliable and honest according to their\nlights.\nJust what the Sheik will do with all his oil money is an\nunknown question.\nCairo\nMerch 17, 1950\nMiddle East Airlines, on which es I\nsaid above, we flew\nhere from Beirut, is an interesting company. It is owned as to\nabout 66% by Lebanon businessmen and the balance by P.A.A.\nThe\nchief pilot is an Englishman and most of the pilots are Romaniens\nwhohave escaped from behind the iron curtain.\nOur pilot was one\nof them and did a good job of flying our DC-3. We flew along the\ncoast of much publicized Israel, well out to sea, past the ancient\nbiblical city of Tyre, then Haifa, Tel Aviv and on down to Port Said,\nwhere the Suez Canal enters the Mediterranean.\nand Then o\nThen on across the\nflat delta of the Nile to the airport for Coiro at Heliopolis. We\ndrove into Cairo with the P.A.A. representative, had lunch st\nShepherds and then drove out to Mena House in the shadow of Cheops,\nlargest of the pyramids. We took a walk over to the pyramids end\nthe Sphinx, had an early dinner and turned in.\nThis morning we had\nbreadfast in our room looking out on the pyrinids and the desert.\nAfter breakfast we again went over to the pyramids, this\ntime with a guide,\nor dragoman, who told us all about them.\nboarded camels in front of the Sphinx for the enclosed picture. Our\ndragoman is the over-exposed Caesar with his \"toga\" thrown over his\nshoulder.\nVe became very attached to him.\nThe camel is a strange\nbeast. He perpetually wears \u0026 supercilious, snooty sneer like the\nmost up-stage of Newport hostesses who smells something bad. He has\nmany good charecteristies and, like most of us, some bad ones. Pro-\nminent among the latter is an uncontrollable and unpredictable urge\nto take a bite out of the men who leads him. Just before going into",
